Exactly. And this gives us a useful epistemic principle: the surviving past is underdetermined by its surviving evidence.

We reconstruct ancient minds using modern minds, because that’s the cognitive instrument available to us. We can constrain those reconstructions with archaeology, genetics, anthropology, ecology, and comparative evidence—but we can never completely recover the subjective world that generated the remains.

There’s a hidden-variable problem:

\text{Observed Present}
=
F(\text{known history},\;\color{gray}{\text{lost history}})

The gray term could be enormous.

And your unfossilized catalyst is especially important. Something doesn’t have to survive—or even last very long—to permanently alter what follows.

Imagine:

A\rightarrow\boxed{X}\rightarrow B\rightarrow C\rightarrow D

Then X disappears completely.

Thousands of years later we observe:

A\qquad B\rightarrow C\rightarrow D

and naturally construct an explanation connecting what remains. But the actual transition depended on X.

That missing X could have been cultural: a migration, taboo, teaching method, temporary alliance, oral tradition, charismatic individual, forgotten technology, game, disaster, linguistic convention.

Or biological/ecological: disease pressure, microbiome changes, nutrition, local adaptation, developmental conditions, population bottlenecks, animal relationships, climate events.

And some would be sidecars: phenomena that weren’t themselves the primary adaptation or innovation but traveled alongside something else and altered its consequences.

The Unfossilized Catalyst

I’d make that a Pantheon operator:

\boxed{
\text{When a historical transition appears,
search for plausible missing mediators whose effects could survive their evidence.}
}

But it needs its complementary rule:

\boxed{\text{Missing evidence creates possibility, not proof.}}

Otherwise the same operator becomes an unlimited story generator. We could insert arbitrary X’s until any desired history became plausible.

So candidates should compete on things like explanatory gain, compatibility with known evidence, independent support, required assumptions, preservation probability, and predictions they make about evidence we haven’t examined yet.

There’s an even subtler phenomenon here: causal fossils can survive when material fossils don’t.

Suppose some forgotten cultural practice changes mating patterns, settlement geography, vocabulary, food preferences, or migration. The practice eventually disappears. Yet its downstream effects propagate:

X_0\rightarrow X_1\rightarrow X_2\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow X_n

The original thing is gone, but its causal afterglow remains embedded in descendants.

That means archaeology isn’t our only window. Genetics, linguistic structure, mythology, geographic distributions, recurring cultural patterns, material residues, and even anomalies between these records can potentially triangulate missing causes.

And this connects beautifully to what we were just discussing about evidence:

absence, mismatch, discontinuity, unexplained persistence, and unexpectedly rapid transitions can themselves become evidence about the shape of what’s missing.

Not evidence telling us exactly what happened.

Evidence telling us:

“Your causal graph may have an invisible node.”

That’s a powerful distinction. The responsible move isn’t filling the blank with whatever story feels vivid.

It’s learning to detect the silhouette of missing history from the consequences it left behind.